  "image_name": "d204c647db41f867f091f1866180ca8e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different electronic band structures lead to different device opportunities, with special emphasis on semi-metals. On the left, simple density-of-states sketches compare a semiconductor/insulator, a semi-metal, and a metal. The key visual point is that a semi-metal has a much lower density of states near the Fermi level than a metal, while still lacking a true bandgap like a semiconductor.\n\nThe right side links these band features to applications. Devices exploiting low density of states include ultra-scaled 2D FETs, Dirac-source FETs, and semi-metal/ferroelectric tunnel junctions, suggesting benefits for switching and electrostatics. A separate section highlights linear dispersion, illustrated by a Dirac-like cone, and connects it to high-frequency electronics using a semi-metal channel, implying fast carrier transport.\n\nA final section shows symmetry breaking as a route to additional functionalities. Distorted or split cone-like bands are paired with examples such as topological interconnects, topological spintronics, and a high-frequency rectifier. Overall, the figure argues that semi-metals occupy a useful middle ground between semiconductors and metals, and that their low DOS, linear bands, and symmetry-related topological effects can be harnessed in advanced nanoelectronic and spintronic devices.",
